@meta
id: 06-06
title: Fly to a character with f
chapter: 06
type: lesson
skills: find-f
par: 14
minutes: 4
vscode: click between the d and the colon
open: refund.types.ts

@brief
`orderId` is not always known when a refund is opened from the finance
console, so the field has to become optional:

    { orderId?: string, ... }

One character, inserted in one exact place, thirty characters into the line.
This is the most common movement problem in editing code, and it has a
two-key answer.

Look at where the `?` goes. It is immediately before a colon — and the
character you need is nearly always sitting next to a piece of punctuation
you can name.

@keys
| `f` | find: the next occurrence of the character you type next |
| `fx` | jump forward onto the next `x` on this line |
| `i` | insert to the left of the cursor |

@hints
Do not count characters and do not press `l` thirty times. Name the
punctuation you are aiming at instead.
--
`f` followed by a character jumps the cursor onto the next one of those on
this line. `f:` goes to the next colon.
--
`f:` lands you on the first colon, which is `orderId`'s. `i`, `?`, `Esc`.

@solution
    f : i ? <Esc> :wq<Enter>

Five keystrokes to make the edit, two of them the travel.

`f` only ever searches the line you are on, which is what makes it safe to
fire without looking. It is the reason vim users rarely reach for the mouse
on a long line: the destination is described, not measured. You are not
saying go right thirty-one columns, you are saying go to the colon — and if
someone renames the field tomorrow, that instruction still works.

Pick the rarest punctuation near your target. `f(`, `f{`, `f'`, `f=`, `f<`
tend to be unique or nearly so in a line of TypeScript, while `fe` in a line
of English is a lottery. When your target is not the first match, you have
`;` and counts coming later in this chapter.

@start refund.types.ts
export type RefundArgs = { orderId: string, amount: number, reason: string };
export type RefundResult = { refundId: string, status: RefundStatus };

@expected refund.types.ts
export type RefundArgs = { orderId?: string, amount: number, reason: string };
export type RefundResult = { refundId: string, status: RefundStatus };
